.\" NOTE: this page contains Cyrillic, and it is the example that matters -- read it
.\" with `nroff -Kutf8 -man', not plain `nroff -man', which renders each byte on its
.\" own.  It is the first page in this tree to need that; cmd/README.md SS10 says so.
.TH REV 1
.SH NAME
rev \- reverse lines of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rev
[ file ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rev
copies the named files to the standard output,
reversing the order of characters in every line.
If no file is specified, the standard input is copied.
.PP
.B "The character here is a UTF-8 sequence, not a byte,"
and that is a deliberate divergence from v7 rather than a detail of this port.
The two are the same thing on an ASCII machine; on this one they are not.
This system's text is UTF\-8 throughout \- the console driver, the filesystem and the shell
all carry eight bits \- so
.B "\`привет\'"
is twelve bytes and six letters, and reversing its bytes yields twelve bytes that are not
text at all.
.I Rev
reverses the letters:
.IP "" 8n
.B "$ echo привет мир | rev"
.br
рим тевирп
.PP
The rule is UTF\-8's own and needs no table: a byte
.B 10xxxxxx
is a continuation and travels with the byte that leads it.
Input that is not valid UTF\-8 loses nothing \- a continuation byte with no lead comes out as
a group of its own \- so
.I rev
is safe on arbitrary bytes even though it no longer treats them all alike.
.SH "LONG LINES"
A line longer than
.B 1024
bytes is cut into pieces of that size and each piece is reversed on its own, with a newline
after it.
That is v7's behaviour, at four times v7's buffer.
.PP
.B Note:
the cut
.B backs off to a character boundary,
so a multi-byte letter is never split between two pieces; the bytes not taken begin the next
one.
Without that the reversal above would be a lie for exactly the lines long enough to need it.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Note:
a final line with
.B no newline
is printed, with a newline added.
v7 discarded whatever was in its buffer when it saw end of file, so
.B "printf abc | rev"
produced nothing at all.
.PP
A file that cannot be opened is reported and
.B ends the run,
the remaining arguments not being read.
That is v7's and is left alone.
The exit status is 1.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1), tail(1)
